!!<*yamlover: $defs: chapter>
Metadata first, validation optional
description: a schema gives a node its shape, type, and format — it is not a gate
- |
  A schema in yamlover is **metadata-first**: it tells consumers what a node *is* — its
  shape, type, `format`, presentation — rather than gating what it may be. Validation is
  optional and separate; nothing refuses to load because a schema disagrees.
- |
  Metadata attaches two ways, one per concrete: a directory's `.yo/meta.yo` overlay — a
  JSON-Schema-equivalent written in yamlover — and, inside a file, the `!!<…>` tag, whose
  contents are themselves yamlover: a pointer to a hosted schema
  (`!!<*yamlover: $defs: chapter>`) or an inline schema literal
  (`!!<format: text/x-plantuml>`). Both speak the same vocabulary
  (the [metadata subchapter](::language:model:metadata)).
